\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{clifford}
\tag{draft}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The signed projective adapter preserves products and incidence}

\p{For the two orders in the preceding Convention, define

##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{toCore}
 &(s,c_0,c_1,c_2,c_{12},c_{20},c_{01},c_{012})\\
 &=(s,c_0,c_1,c_{01},c_2,-c_{20},c_{12},c_{012}),\\
\operatorname{toNamed}
 &(r,r_0,r_1,r_{01},r_2,r_{02},r_{12},r_{012})\\
 &=(r,r_0,r_1,r_2,r_{12},-r_{02},r_{01},r_{012}).
\end{aligned}}

They are inverse linear maps on every eight-tuple. The minus sign is exactly
#{e_{20}=-e_{02}}; substituting either displayed tuple into the other proves
both round trips coordinate by coordinate.}

\p{This signed permutation also preserves the Clifford product, rather than
merely being declared to do so. Encode the ascending blades by masks
#{0,\ldots,7}, with bit #{i} representing #{e_i}, and put

##{\sigma=(0,1,2,4,6,5,3,7),\qquad
\epsilon=(1,1,1,1,1,-1,1,1).}

Then the #{i}-th named blade is
#{S_i=\epsilon_iC_{\sigma_i}}. For masks identified with subsets of
#{\{0,1,2\}}, set

##{g(m,n)=(-1)^{\#\{(i,j)\in m\times n:i>j\}}
          \prod_{i\in m\cap n}q_i,
\qquad(q_0,q_1,q_2)=(0,1,1).}

The strict-inversion formula of \ref{fcap-002O} gives
#{C_mC_n=g(m,n)C_{m\mathbin{\triangle}n}}. If
#{\sigma_k=\sigma_i\mathbin{\triangle}\sigma_j}, it follows that

##{S_iS_j=\epsilon_i\epsilon_j\epsilon_k
g(\sigma_i,\sigma_j)S_k.}

This checks every one of the #{64} basis pairs, including the zero products
caused by a repeated #{e_0}; bilinearity proves multiplication compatibility
for arbitrary tuples. Addition, scalar multiplication, and the unit are
preserved immediately.}

\p{The permutation #{\sigma} preserves grade. Reversal multiplies a
grade-#{k} blade by #{(-1)^{k(k-1)/2}}, so it commutes with the adapter.
For exterior multiplication, the same crossing sign applies to disjoint
masks and both sides vanish when masks overlap. Hence the adapter also
preserves reversal and exterior multiplication. It therefore preserves
inverses wherever they exist and, in particular,

##{\operatorname{toCore}(MX\widetilde M)
=\operatorname{toCore}(M)\operatorname{toCore}(X)
 \widetilde{\operatorname{toCore}(M)}.}

Under the ordered basis of \ref{fcap-002P}, these equations give the
corresponding abstract-coordinate commuting square. They do not supply a
named executable coordinate type or software-storage theorem.}

\p{For the projective representatives of the preceding Convention, direct
exterior multiplication gives

##{L(d,a,b)\wedge P(w,x,y)=(dw+ax+by)I.}

Since #{I} is a nonzero basis blade, the point lies on the line precisely
when #{dw+ax+by=0}; no inverse pseudoscalar is used. This agrees with
Sokolov's incidence equation
\citet{(37), p. 25}{sokolov2013foundations}. The four coordinate rows are

##{\begin{aligned}
[L(d,a,b)]_S&=(0,d,a,b,0,0,0,0),\\
[L(d,a,b)]_C&=(0,d,a,0,b,0,0,0),\\
[P(w,x,y)]_S&=(0,0,0,0,w,x,y,0),\\
[P(w,x,y)]_C&=(0,0,0,y,0,-x,w,0).
\end{aligned}}

Thus the abstract incidence calculation and both coordinate presentations
agree through the full adapter.}

\p{The ideal-direction sign is fixed by the same convention. Namely,

##{e_0\wedge(ue_1+ve_2)
=ue_{01}+ve_{02}=ue_{01}-ve_{20}=P(0,-v,u).}

The input pair #{(u,v)} is a line normal, while the resulting homogeneous
point has direction #{(-v,u)}. Direction #{(u,v)} itself is represented by
#{P(0,u,v)}. This distinction keeps the algebraic encoding separate from a
drawn arrow-orientation convention.}

\p{The result synthesizes Sokolov's projective encoding with the explicit
blade kernel of \ref{fcap-002O} and the finite coordinate refinement of
\ref{fcap-002P}. FCAP's current exterior-basis transport is noncomputable and
does not yet expose these named conversion functions, an executable
multiplication kernel, or compatibility theorems. Those implementation and
external storage-order claims remain separate work.}
